    {
      "question": "Is it better to close a credit card or leave it open with a zero balance?",
      "answer": "Keeping an unused card open can help maintain a higher credit score, especially an old card with a positive payment history. This preserves your total available credit limit and keeps your utilization ratio lower. Closing the card might be a good decision if the account carries annual fees or poor terms that outweigh the benefits, or if it helps you avoid accumulating debt you cannot pay off. Suppose you keep an account open with a zero balance. The CFPB advises monitoring monthly statements to protect against identity theft and catch unexpected fees."
    },
    {
      "question": "How long does closing a credit card hurt your score?",
      "answer": "The CFPB notes that any change to your credit score resulting from a card closure may be temporary or minor, but the CFPB does not specify a set number of months or years. The full impact varies based on other factors across your credit profile. Check the [CFPB guidance page](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/does-it-hurt-my-credit-to-close-a-credit-card-en-1231/) directly for updates on credit reporting rules."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does closing multiple credit cards hurt your score?",
      "answer": "Closing multiple cards can have a larger negative effect on your credit score because each closure removes an additional credit limit from your total available credit."
    },
    {
      "question": "What happens if I close a credit card with a zero balance?",
      "answer": "Closing a card with a zero balance removes that card's available credit limit from your overall borrowing capacity without changing the dollar amount of debt you carry on other cards. If you carry balances on any other credit cards, your total credit utilization ratio will increase, which can cause a temporary or minor decrease in your credit score."
